\begin{abstract}
We develop a quantitative, structure-preserving comparison between two
\emph{a priori} unrelated cost measures on functions: \emph{valuation depth}, an
additive (max-plus) complexity measure native to non-Archimedean computation, and
the \emph{Lipschitz constants of tropical valuation objects}, a multiplicative
growth measure native to tropical algebra and ultrametric analysis. The central
thesis is that valuation depth is the discrete logarithm of a tropical Lipschitz
constant: the exponential map $d \mapsto \base^d$ carries the max-plus depth law
$\vdepth(f\circ g)\le \max(\vdepth f, \vdepth g)+1$ to the multiplicative tropical
Lipschitz law $v(f^{[n]})\le C^n v$, and the discrete logarithm $\Natlog$ inverts
it. We organize the bridge into three layers: an axiomatic theory of valuation
depth with a max-plus composition law, an infinite strict complexity hierarchy
$\VAL_k$, and an iteration-stability theorem; a category of tropical valuation
objects with a norm-faithful \emph{valuation reconstruction functor} to
ultrametric seminormed objects that transfers Lipschitz bounds with no loss of
constant; and the comparison itself, the identity
$\base^{\max(a,b)+1} = \base\cdot\max(\base^a,\base^b)$, which exhibits the
exponential as a $1$-Lipschitz comparison functor between the two laws. We close
with applications to certified robustness, post-quantum security-gap transfer, and
Hensel iteration complexity, and with testable conjectures.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring phenomenon in mathematics is that two theories obey ``the same'' law
up to a substitution of operations: where one has $+$ and $\max$, the other has
$\times$ and $\cdot$. Whenever this happens, a logarithm typically mediates. This
paper makes one such mediation precise and quantitative.

On one side is \emph{valuation depth}, a complexity measure modeled on
non-Archimedean (p-adic) arithmetic, whose defining feature is that
\emph{combining} two computations costs the \emph{maximum} of their costs plus a
constant, never the sum. This max-plus signature stems from the ultrametric
inequality $\|a+b\|\le\max(\|a\|,\|b\|)$, which removes carry propagation. On the
other side are \emph{tropical valuation objects}: ordered idempotent semiring-like
structures whose addition is maximum, $a\oplus b=\max(a,b)$, carrying a valuation
$v$ whose Lipschitz maps are multiplicative, $v(f(x))\le C\,v(x)$, with iteration
producing $C^n$.

Our contribution is a faithful comparison: the additive depth law and the
multiplicative Lipschitz law are interchanged by the exponential/logarithm pair,
exactly and without losing constants; the tropical world reconstructs the
ultrametric world through a norm-faithful functor; and bounds proved cheaply
transfer with the same constants to ultrametric certified guarantees used in
cryptography and machine learning. All results stated below have been formalized
and machine-checked.

\section{Valuation depth: the additive (max-plus) side}

\subsection{The depth measure}

\begin{definition}[Valuation depth measure]
Let $\alpha,\beta$ be semirings. A \emph{valuation depth measure} assigns to each
$f:\alpha\to\beta$ a natural number $\vdepth(f)$ with
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\vdepth(\lambda x.\,0)=0$;
\item $\vdepth(\lambda x.\,f(x)+g(x))\le\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth g)+1$;
\item $\vdepth(\lambda x.\,f(x)\cdot g(x))\le\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth g)+1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Derived depth bounds]
$\vdepth(f\cdot f)\le\vdepth f+1$ (squaring), $\vdepth(f+f)\le\vdepth f+1$
(doubling), and $\vdepth(f+g+h)\le\max(\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth g)+1,\vdepth h)+1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Specialize the binary axioms with $g=f$ and use $\max(d,d)=d$; for the triple
sum apply axiom (2) twice and finish over $\N$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The composition (max-plus) law}

\begin{definition}[Ultrametric composition law]
A depth measure on $\alpha\to\alpha$ satisfies the \emph{ultrametric composition
law} if $\vdepth(f\circ g)\le\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth g)+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Triple composition and one-step iteration]
Under the composition law,
\[ \vdepth(f\circ g\circ h)\le\max(\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth g)+1,\vdepth h)+1, \]
and $\vdepth(f^{[n+1]})\le\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth(f^{[n]}))+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reassociate $f\circ g\circ h=(f\circ g)\circ h$ and apply the law twice; for
iteration write $f^{[n+1]}=f\circ f^{[n]}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Complexity classes and a strict hierarchy}

\begin{definition}[Depth classes]
$\VAL_k=\{f:\vdepth(f)\le k\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Lattice of classes]
$\VAL_k\subseteq\VAL_{k+1}$; if $f,g\in\VAL_k$ then $f+g,f\cdot g\in\VAL_{k+1}$;
and $\bigcup_k\VAL_k$ is everything.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Depth witness]
A \emph{depth witness} for level $k$ is a function $w$ with $\vdepth(w)=k+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Strict hierarchy]
If a depth witness for level $k$ exists, then $\VAL_k\subsetneq\VAL_{k+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$w\in\VAL_{k+1}\setminus\VAL_k$; equality would force $\vdepth(w)\le k$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Ultrametric Lipschitz data and iteration stability}

\begin{definition}[Ultrametric Lipschitz data]
A triple of an integer $\expo\in\Z$, a boolean non-expansiveness flag, and the
consistency law $\text{flag}\Leftrightarrow\expo\ge0$. Composition takes the
minimum, $\expo(f\circ g)=\min(\expo f,\expo g)$, with
$\mathrm{iter}(f,0)=f$, $\mathrm{iter}(f,n+1)=\mathrm{compose}(\mathrm{iter}(f,n),f)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Ultrametric robustness / iteration stability]
$\expo(\mathrm{iter}(f,n))=\expo(f)$ for all $n$: the exponent is invariant under
iteration.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$ with $\min(\expo f,\expo f)=\expo f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classical--ultrametric gap]
For $L\ge2$ and $n\ge2$, $L^n/L\ge L$: classical iterates blow up while the
ultrametric exponent is unchanged.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$L^n=L^{n-1}\cdot L$; cancel and bound $L^{n-1}\ge L$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Locality speedup and Hensel complexity}

\begin{theorem}[Ultrametric locality speedup]
For $n\ge2$ there are depths $\mathrm{classical}\ge\log_2 n$ and
$\mathrm{ultra}=1$ with $\mathrm{classical}\ge\mathrm{ultra}$, and the gap
$\log_2 n$ is unbounded.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Hensel quadratic convergence and complexity]
For a certified Hensel--Newton sequence with $\mathrm{prec}(n+1)\ge2\,\mathrm{prec}(n)$
and $\mathrm{prec}(0)\ge1$: $\mathrm{prec}(n)\ge2^n$; $\lceil\log_2 n\rceil+1$
steps suffice for $n$ digits; and $\log_2 n+1<n$ for $n\ge3$. Concretely $11$
steps give $\ge1024$ digits and $21$ steps give $\ge10^6$ digits.
\end{theorem}

\section{Tropical valuation objects: the multiplicative side}

\begin{definition}[Tropical valuation object]
A linearly ordered $(R,\le)$ with $0,1$, operations $\oplus,\otimes,\max$, where
$\max$ is the join, $\otimes$ a commutative monoid with unit $1$ and absorbing
$0$, $a\oplus0=a$, and the tropical principle $a\oplus b=\max(a,b)$. A
\emph{carrier} additionally has $v:K\to\N$ with $v(x\oplus y)\le\max(v(x),v(y))$
and $v(x\otimes y)=v(x)\cdot v(y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ultrametric seminorm object]
A type $\alpha$ with operations and a norm $\|\cdot\|:\alpha\to\N$ obeying the
strong triangle inequality $\|x+y\|\le\max(\|x\|,\|y\|)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Valuation reconstruction]
The functor sending a carrier $X$ to the ultrametric object on $X.K$ with the
same operations and with norm equal to $v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Faithfulness of reconstruction]
$\|x\|_{\rec(X)}=v(x)$ and $\|x\otimes y\|_{\rec(X)}=v(x)\cdot v(y)$; the
reconstructed object is ultrametric. The reconstruction is functorial (preserves
identities and composition).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The norm is defined as $v$; the laws are the carrier axioms.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Lipschitz predicates]
$\TropLip_C(f)$ iff $v(f(x))\le C\,v(x)$ for all $x$; $\UltraLip_C(f)$ iff
$\|f(x)\|\le C\,\|x\|$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp Lipschitz transfer]
$\TropLip_C(f)$ on $X$ implies $\UltraLip_C(f)$ on $\rec(X)$, with the same
constant $C$; nonexpansiveness transfers likewise.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The ultrametric norm is the valuation, so the two inequalities coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Iterated tropical and ultrametric rates]
If $v(f(x))\le C\,v(x)$ then $v(f^{[n]}(x))\le C^n v(x)$ for all $n,x$, and the
same $C^n$ bound holds for the reconstructed norm.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$: base $C^0=1$; step uses $f^{[n+1]}=f\circ f^{[n]}$,
monotonicity, and $C\cdot C^n=C^{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Application transfers]
With the same constants: quantum-certified radius transfer, post-quantum
security-gap transfer ($v(y\ominus\mathrm{secret})\ge\mathrm{gap}$), Lipschitz
certified robustness ($\|f(x)\|\le L\,\|\mathrm{center}\|$), and max-stability
$\|x\oplus y\|\le\max(\|x\|,\|y\|)$.
\end{remark}

\section{The comparison functor: depth $=$ log of tropical Lipschitz constant}

Fix $\base>1$.

\begin{lemma}[Exponential intertwines max-plus and multiplicative]
For all $a,b\in\N$, $\base^{\max(a,b)+1}=\base\cdot\max(\base^a,\base^b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$d\mapsto\base^d$ is strictly monotone, so
$\base^{\max(a,b)}=\max(\base^a,\base^b)$; multiply by $\base$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Comparison of laws]
Let $T(f):=\base^{\vdepth(f)}$ be the \emph{tropical shadow}. Then
$\vdepth(f\circ g)\le\max(\vdepth f,\vdepth g)+1$ holds iff
$T(f\circ g)\le\base\cdot\max(T(f),T(g))$, and
$\vdepth=\Natlog_{\base}\circ T$ recovers depth.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the strictly monotone $\base^{(-)}$ and the previous lemma; invert with
$\Natlog_{\base}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Faithful comparison]
$d\mapsto\base^d$ and $C\mapsto\Natlog_{\base}C$ form a monotone Galois pair,
exact on powers of $\base$. For $\base\ge2$, $d_1<d_2\Rightarrow
\base^{d_1}<\base^{d_2}$, so the depth order embeds isometrically into the shadow
order and $\VAL_k$ maps to the strictly increasing classes $\{f:T(f)\le\base^k\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strict monotonicity and the round-trip identity
$\Natlog_{\base}(\base^d)=d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Iteration under the comparison]
Depth iteration gives depth $O(n)$, whose shadow $\base^{O(n)}$ matches the
tropical rate $C^n$. The stable-exponent law corresponds to the multiplicative
fixed point $C=1$ ($1^n=1$): ``no blow-up'' and ``non-expansive stays
non-expansive'' are the same theorem through $\exp/\log$.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Depth evaluation.} Bottom-up fold of an expression tree;
internal node returns $\max(\text{children})+1$; $O(\text{size})$.
\item \textbf{Shadow and inverse.} $b^d$ and $\lfloor\log_b s\rfloor$; exact on
powers; $O(\log s)$.
\item \textbf{Iterated rate.} $C^n$ by fast exponentiation; depth
$\approx n\log_b C$; $O(\log n)$.
\item \textbf{Hensel step count.} $\lfloor\log_2 D\rfloor+1$ for $D$ digits;
$O(\log D)$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Applications}
Certified robustness (a tropical Lipschitz bound becomes an ultrametric
robustness radius with the same constant), post-quantum security (gap
preservation), fast non-Archimedean arithmetic (depth $1$ vs. $\log_2 n$;
$O(\log n)$ Hensel lifting), and max-plus scheduling (depth $=$ makespan, shadow
$=$ throughput).

\section{Discussion}
The comparison is deliberately quantitative: constants are preserved and the
exponential is an order-embedding comparison, so one may prove in the cheap,
carry-free world and quote in the expensive, adversarial world. The ``+1'' per
depth layer is absorbed into the multiplicative factor $\base$, which makes the
comparison exact for iteration and carries an additive defect of size $1$ for
genuinely parallel composition.

\section{Future directions}
\textbf{C1 (Fekete limit).} $\vdepth(a^{[n]})/(n+1)$ converges to a tropical
Lyapunov exponent; prove $\vdepth(a^{[m+n]})\le\vdepth(a^{[m]})+\vdepth(a^{[n]})+1$
and apply a discrete Fekete lemma. \textbf{C2 (Strictness transfer).} A depth
witness separating $\VAL_k\subsetneq\VAL_{k+1}$ transfers under $T$ to strict
separation of rate classes. \textbf{C3 (Two-sided defect).} A universal $c$ with
$|\log_\base T(\mathrm{comp}(a,b))-\max(\vdepth a,\vdepth b)|\le c$, with $c=1$
sharp; balanced trees of $n$ leaves have depth
$\le\lceil\log_2 n\rceil\cdot(\text{per-leaf}+1)$. \textbf{C4 (Naturality).} $T$
extends to a functor on morphism categories and the depth$\leftrightarrow$log-rate
equality is a natural isomorphism, $\log_\base\circ T\circ\varphi_* =
\varphi_*\circ\vdepth$.

\section{Conclusion}
Valuation depth and tropical Lipschitz constants are two coordinate systems for
one phenomenon, related by $\exp$ and $\log$. The additive max-plus depth law and
the multiplicative tropical rate law are interchanged exactly by the exponential
comparison functor; valuation reconstruction turns tropical data into ultrametric
norms without loss of constant; and the resulting transfers certify robustness and
security guarantees computed in the cheaper world.

